<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-unions-of-topologies" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Unions of topologies
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Give an example of two topologies on a set whose union is not a topology on that set.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m> X = \set{ a , b , c } </m>, and consider the topologies
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \tau_{ 1 } \amp = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ a } , X } \amp \tau_{ 2 } \amp = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ b } , X }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      Then <m> \tau_{ 1 } \cup \tau_{ 2 } = \set{ \emptyset , \set{ a } , \set{ b } , X } </m> is not a topology on <m> X </m>, since it is not stable under unions. Indeed, <m> \set{ a } , \set{ b } \in \tau_{ 1 } \cup \tau_{ 2 } </m>, but <m> \set{ a } \cup \set{ b } = \set{ a , b } \notin \tau_{ 1 } \cup \tau_{ 2 } </m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>
